          "type": "assistant",
          "text": "There are several good command-line tools for testing WebSocket servers:\n\n## 1. **wscat** (easiest, Node-based)\n\n```bash\n# Install\nnpm install -g wscat\n\n# Connect to WebSocket server\nwscat -c ws://localhost:8080\n\n# With authentication header\nwscat -c ws://localhost:8080 -H \"Authorization: Bearer token\"\n\n# WSS (secure WebSocket)\nwscat -c wss://your-domain.com\n```\n\nOnce connected, type messages and press Enter to send them.\n\n## 2. **websocat** (most powerful, Rust-based)\n\n```bash\n# Install (Linux)\nwget https://github.com/vi/websocat/releases/download/v1.12.0/websocat.x86_64-unknown-linux-musl\nchmod +x websocat.x86_64-unknown-linux-musl\nsudo mv websocat.x86_64-unknown-linux-musl /usr/local/bin/websocat\n\n# Connect\nwebsocat ws://localhost:8080\n\n# Send specific message and close\necho '{\"type\":\"ping\"}' | websocat ws://localhost:8080\n\n# Interactive with headers\nwebsocat -H \"Authorization: Bearer token\" ws://localhost:8080\n```\n\n## 3. **curl** (newer versions support WebSocket)\n\n```bash\n# curl 7.86.0+ supports WebSocket\ncurl --no-buffer \\\n     --header \"Connection: Upgrade\" \\\n     --header \"Upgrade: websocket\" \\\n     --header \"Sec-WebSocket-Version: 13\" \\\n     --header \"Sec-WebSocket-Key: SGVsbG8sIHdvcmxkIQ==\" \\\n     http://localhost:8080\n```\n\n## For your use-ai server specifically:\n\n```bash\n# Install wscat\nnpm install -g wscat\n\n# Connect to your server\nwscat -c ws://localhost:8080\n\n# Then send Socket.IO messages (your server uses Socket.IO)\n# Note: Socket.IO has its own protocol, so raw WebSocket clients \n# may not work perfectly - you'll see Socket.IO handshake messages\n```\n\n**Recommendation:** Start with **wscat** for quick testing. For production testing or scripting, use **websocat**.",
